WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.300
A complete beginner just built a working racing game on his MacBook using only ChatGPT. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 227. Safety tests before a model ships, robots taught by video, shopping agents in ChatGPT, then a deep dive, cool stuff and try this, and quick bits fill the rest. Someone with no coding history at all decided in August, a Reddit post says, to make a racing game. He asked ChatGPT, the Chatbot you type questions into for help.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:34.060
It pointed him to Godot, a free game engine that works on computers and phones.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.880
Think of Godot as a giant box of Lego made for video games.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.700
You snap pieces together instead of carving every brick by hand.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.940
Across the next two months, weekend tinkering became a daily habit that now takes six to eight

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:47.880
hours after work.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.400
The coastline alone ate two full days of a loop that feels like art class.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.540
He would launch around, say which bit looked off, receive fresh code, and try again.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:02.840
The water uses shaders, which are tiny recipes that tell every pixel how light should sit

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.820
on a surface, like a makeup tutorial for the ocean.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:10.740
Foam is painted on with generated textures, the wrapping paper that sits on top of a

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:11.000
shape.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:17.160
Splash motion was built in Blender, a free tool for making 3D movement, then imported into

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.540
Godot as moving textures.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.300
He first tried a recording of a real engine, and it felt off inside the game, so chat GPT

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.940
helped him invent a custom synth.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:31.160
A synth is like a keyboard that invents a noise from scratch, note by note, instead of

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.640
playing a tape of a real motor.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:38.680
Background audio came from 11 labs or Adobe Firefly, which are tools that can invent voices,

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.080
whooshes, and room tone from a description.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:46.440
The vehicle is a ready-made stock model that carries a free license, meaning anyone is

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.020
allowed to use that 3D car.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:53.360
He built buildings and props from type descriptions or pictures the AI drew as references.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:00.140
A few scenery pieces arrived as full 3D models from TRIPO AI, Codex, and AI helper for

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:05.120
code and files, then help slim down both the meshes and textures so Godot could run them.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.140
Meshes are the chicken wire frames under a 3D object, slimming them is like shrinking

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.820
a stuffed backpack until a laptop can carry it.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:20.440
He began on GPT 5.6 Sol with a $20 plan, then in November switched to GPT 6 Astro with

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:21.680
a $500 plan.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:28.420
He tests on an M1 Pro, MacBook Pro, and an iPhone 15 Pro, building both at once because the phone

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:34.340
build has to run leaner. That is like mixing the same song twice, once for stadium speakers and

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:40.720
once for tiny earbuds. Attention sits on steering feel, how the car bumps into the world, and whether

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.940
the look and the sound work together while moving. He plans sprints, circuit races, and rivals, and a

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.860
The story wants those modes actually run.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.520
A police chase comes first, making a real game used to mean a long education or a crew,

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.680
and that wall is a lot lower now.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:01.300
If a homemade game has ever been on your wish list, this is proof you can start.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:06.580
Go to the chat GPT website, start a new chat, and type that you have never coded before.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:16.920
Then ask it to help you make a simple 2D racing game in the Free Good O engine, and to give

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.260
Not a full 3D world yet.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.660
Save your progress in a notebook so you can keep going tomorrow.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:27.620
Researchers at Anthropic, writing on the company's engineering blog, put out 10 practical

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:31.280
rules for building safety checks on artificial intelligence, or AI.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.460
Unlocking your phone with your face is a tiny gate.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.360
The phone is checking that the right person is about to do something that matters.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.320
These rules are a much bigger version of that gate.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:46.560
Instead of guarding one phone, they guard against a model that might give bad advice or cause

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.900
harm. This part is a little tricky, but the simple version is enough. People who know

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:56.920
the topic and people who know the machinery should both help write the tests, not one group

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:01.320
alone. That is like a group project where the history student and the science student

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:06.960
both help write the quiz, so it is not just one person's blind spot. Spell out the exact

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:12.460
thing you are measuring before any test begins. The post-barrels from psychology's replication

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:17.800
reforms from a decade ago, including pre-registration of hypotheses and analysis plans.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:23.660
A hypothesis is the guess you are checking. An analysis plan is how you will score the results.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:29.180
Pre-registration means you show that plan first, like handing your teacher the quiz questions

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:34.180
before anyone takes the test, so you cannot quietly change the rules after you see the answers.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.080
A test should not fall over just because someone rephrases the question.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.740
Picture a student who memorizes the answer keys phrasing instead of actually learning

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:43.420
the material.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:48.300
Run the check more than one way, since scores can shift when items are multiple choice

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.340
versus open-ended.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.540
Same pop quiz, two formats.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:53.480
Bubble sheets can hide a guess.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.580
A blank page shows whether the idea is really there.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:02.240
Clear proof should exist before a new model ships that it will not help a person harm themselves

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.180
or other people.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:07.860
The evidence sits in the open rather than a company simply asking the world to believe

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:08.040
it.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:13.640
Once you see the steps, a safety test stops feeling like secret lab magic and starts feeling

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.940
like a checklist you could explain to a classmate.

00:05:16.864 --> 00:05:22.184
Praxis 1, an open source model from Runway, was trained on millions of videos and can now

00:05:22.184 --> 00:05:24.584
control robots, the decoder reports.

00:05:25.264 --> 00:05:27.444
Open source here means the recipe is public.

00:05:28.164 --> 00:05:33.364
Runway released open weights and source code under the Apache 2.0 license, a permission

00:05:33.364 --> 00:05:36.304
slip that lets anyone use, share, and build on it.

00:05:36.304 --> 00:05:41.084
Wates are the millions of little knobs the model learned, like the muscle memory in a

00:05:41.084 --> 00:05:43.384
dancer's body after watching a million clips.

00:05:43.884 --> 00:05:48.784
It uses knowledge from video training for robot control and leads the world action model

00:05:48.784 --> 00:05:52.204
category on Nvidia's RoboLab 120 benchmark.

00:05:52.764 --> 00:05:54.964
A benchmark is a scored obstacle course.

00:05:55.544 --> 00:06:00.044
World action model means AI that turns what it sees into a physical next step.

00:06:00.464 --> 00:06:03.604
It cannot yet plan complex action sequences on its own.

00:06:03.604 --> 00:06:08.684
Instead of writing thousands of lines of robot code, the model uses what it learned from

00:06:08.684 --> 00:06:12.224
watching things move to tell a robot arm or body what to do next.

00:06:12.824 --> 00:06:17.724
That is learning a dance from YouTube, then your arms copy the next move, without yet

00:06:17.724 --> 00:06:19.284
choreographing a whole recital.

00:06:19.804 --> 00:06:24.784
It is free to download if you have a decent computer, and it is one path from tools that make

00:06:24.784 --> 00:06:28.144
cool videos to tools that can help robots do real tasks.

00:06:28.144 --> 00:06:34.384
Go to Hugging Face, a site where people share AI models, search for Praxis 1, and scroll

00:06:34.384 --> 00:06:38.704
the examples until you see a robot arm stacking blocks after only watching video.

00:06:39.324 --> 00:06:46.804
Open AI launch Chat GPT wallet on November 13th, 2026, so special agents inside Chat GPT

00:06:46.804 --> 00:06:51.064
